1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,120
Hey everyone.  We have covered division misalignment, transfer pricing on functional silos.

2
00:00:05,280 --> 00:00:09,600
We wrap up by taking a look at budget battles with our fictional firm.

3
00:00:09,760 --> 00:00:22,000
Let's jump into it at NexTech annual budget season as a high stakes negotiation because division managers know that hitting their budget target means a bonus and if they miss it, then they don't get a payout.

4
00:00:23,120 --> 00:00:25,520
We see several things occur when budgets are set.

5
00:00:25,960 --> 00:00:31,160
First, when budget targets are tied to compensation, managers often reduce the risk of their targets.

6
00:00:31,320 --> 00:00:34,440
Why add more to your plate and affect your bonus?

7
00:00:34,680 --> 00:00:37,720
This also means that managers will lowball estimates.

8
00:00:38,200 --> 00:00:42,840
Since we are aiming low, managers will go for reachable rather than realistic forecasts.

9
00:00:42,920 --> 00:00:47,080
They will forecast what they can defend, whether or not they can achieve more or not.

10
00:00:47,400 --> 00:00:51,960
This often involves political skill as they want to ensure that they meet their goals.

11
00:00:51,960 --> 00:00:55,960
And bonuses are as opposed to to create an efficiency and firm profit.

12
00:00:56,040 --> 00:01:03,080
It may even involve some departments politicking to raise goals of other departments while they lower theirs.

13
00:01:03,480 --> 00:01:07,320
Lastly, managers may view performance planning as tactical positioning.

14
00:01:07,320 --> 00:01:11,480
Managers are attempting to play the political game by setting expectations low.

15
00:01:11,640 --> 00:01:17,000
This can cause a resource misallocation or as low projections will mean a low budget.

16
00:01:17,240 --> 00:01:22,000
This also creates a problem for the firm as it is built on estimates and projection.

17
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:25,480
And if those are faulty, then the firm's plans are also faulty.

18
00:01:25,720 --> 00:01:31,400
Departments that create such inaccuracies can create a system of mistrust and breakdowns and coordination.

19
00:01:31,800 --> 00:01:38,120
Finally, when departments encourage gaming, they tell their employees to hit targets instead of finding better ways to perform.

20
00:01:38,680 --> 00:01:42,600
These incentives reward manipulation and not improvement.

21
00:01:42,840 --> 00:01:46,280
In many organizations, budget planning is more than coordination.

22
00:01:46,520 --> 00:01:48,680
It serves as a performance contract.

23
00:01:49,280 --> 00:01:55,200
Divisional leaders know that their compensation depends on meeting or exceeding assigned targets.

24
00:01:55,280 --> 00:02:01,280
This creates a powerful incentive to not just plan, but to negotiate the most reachable goal.

25
00:02:01,520 --> 00:02:09,040
The result is often a budget process shaped less by expected performance and more by tactical positioning.

26
00:02:09,920 --> 00:02:20,360
So when incentives are tied to budget targets, they often backfire because they encourage managers to manipulate the system by shaping the numbers and not improving the business.

27
00:02:21,640 --> 00:02:27,880
As we've seen, when the wrong incentives are in place, managers will seek to maximize rewards and minimize their risk.

28
00:02:28,520 --> 00:02:49,600
Some common ways that they achieve this are through sandbagging, that is intentionally creating forecasts in their favor, channel stuffing, or pushing sales forward to create more revenue than what is realistically possible, moving expenses to a future quarter, holding off on deals so they can achieve a future goal, and avoiding collaboration with other departments to protect key performance indicators.

29
00:02:49,920 --> 00:02:52,640
This type of behavior creates issues for the firm.

30
00:02:52,800 --> 00:02:59,360
As managers and departments create budget manipulations, they may lead to under or overproduction for the firm.

31
00:02:59,760 --> 00:03:04,000
More finger pointing and distrust creates issues for long term planning.

32
00:03:04,720 --> 00:03:12,240
When rewards are concentrated at a specific threshold, say $4 million in profit, managers begin to behave strategically.

33
00:03:12,480 --> 00:03:20,440
They this includes sandbagging under stating those projections, channel stuffing, accelerating revenue and cost shifting that is delaying expenses.

34
00:03:21,080 --> 00:03:23,080
These behaviors are not errors.

35
00:03:23,160 --> 00:03:26,840
They are logical responses to flawed incentive structures.

36
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:33,880
In economic terms, managers are optimizing under constraints but not in the firm's best interests.

37
00:03:35,080 --> 00:03:37,160
So the cost of manipulation is real.

38
00:03:37,560 --> 00:03:40,320
Products are mistimed and forecasts go ignored.

39
00:03:40,320 --> 00:03:44,040
And what looks like success on paper becomes a failure in execution.

40
00:03:44,800 --> 00:03:48,840
Because of distorted forecasts, firms experience many misalignments.

41
00:03:48,840 --> 00:03:54,240
We see planning on flawed numbers and bad decisions cascade into other areas.

42
00:03:54,880 --> 00:04:04,800
The firm experiences these consequences as they overproduce, use more marketing expenditures than necessary, have delayed revenue and have to deal with finger pointing.

43
00:04:05,760 --> 00:04:18,440
Lastly, the firm has to deal with structural and strategic costs such as initiatives derailed by unreliable data and decision makers optimizing for today and not for long term value of the company.

44
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:25,720
The manipulation of budget inputs distorts not only internal reporting but downstream operations.

45
00:04:26,200 --> 00:04:30,080
Manufacturing may under produce, marketing may mistime campaigns.

46
00:04:30,080 --> 00:04:31,800
Finance may misallocate capital.

47
00:04:32,120 --> 00:04:36,760
The entire organization begins reacting to fiction rather than fact.

48
00:04:37,560 --> 00:04:43,200
These distortions reduce firm level efficiency and undermine strategic alignment economically.

49
00:04:43,200 --> 00:04:47,640
We refer to this as information loss due to incentive misalignment.

50
00:04:48,600 --> 00:04:50,200
So NexTech made a change.

51
00:04:50,520 --> 00:04:57,000
They replaced the bonus cliffs with a linear system rewarding each dollar of performance instead of just hitting a line.

52
00:04:57,480 --> 00:05:06,760
In the old threshold based incentive model, bonuses were all or nothing paid only when performance met a fixed target like the 4 million dollar in profit.

53
00:05:06,920 --> 00:05:15,160
This structure created pressure to manipulate outcomes near the threshold through tactics like sandbagging forecasts, accelerating sales or deferring expenses.

54
00:05:15,640 --> 00:05:24,280
In contrast, NexTech adopted a linear pay for performance model where rewards increase proportionally with results.

55
00:05:24,840 --> 00:05:32,440
Every dollar of profit contributes to compensation, eliminating cliffs and reducing the incentive to to game the system.

56
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:39,480
This shift promotes steady effort, honest forecasting and better alignment between performance and planning.

57
00:05:39,880 --> 00:05:45,320
Simply put, incentives lead to smoother, more strategic behavior.

58
00:05:46,040 --> 00:05:50,680
Threshold based compensation schemes create discontinuities and incentives.

59
00:05:50,680 --> 00:05:56,040
Regions where behavior changes drastically just before or after the payout threshold.

60
00:05:56,200 --> 00:05:58,920
This is known as a kinked structured.

61
00:05:59,240 --> 00:06:07,960
By moving to a linear compensation model where rewards increase smoothly with performance, firms eliminate the incentive to manipulate forecasts.

62
00:06:08,200 --> 00:06:15,800
Linear incentives preserve marginal motivation across the full performance range which tends to produce more honest reporting.

63
00:06:17,160 --> 00:06:20,200
With forecast more honest decisions being more accurate.

64
00:06:20,200 --> 00:06:23,000
We see that strategy started working the way it was meant to.

65
00:06:24,280 --> 00:06:29,640
When linear incentives replace rigid budget based bonuses, behavioral dynamics shift fundamentally.

66
00:06:29,960 --> 00:06:35,240
Managers no longer need to manipulate the timing of revenue or expenses to meet arbitrary targets.

67
00:06:35,320 --> 00:06:41,960
As a result, forecasts become more accurate and departments begin to trust the numbers not just their own, but across the divisions.

68
00:06:42,200 --> 00:06:47,320
This improved data integrity leads to better cross functional coordination and more reliable planning.

69
00:06:47,880 --> 00:06:56,560
Operationally, the company experiences smoother production schedules, better time marketing campaigns and more stable cash flow, thus reducing last minute surprises.

70
00:06:56,960 --> 00:07:01,600
And strategically incentives now align with long term value creation.

71
00:07:01,840 --> 00:07:09,200
Consistent effort and gradual improvement are rewarded, fostering transparency, accountability and organizational trust.

72
00:07:10,080 --> 00:07:11,600
So let's look at some key takeaways.

73
00:07:11,600 --> 00:07:13,480
We know that incentives drive behavior.

74
00:07:13,480 --> 00:07:17,800
If you pay people to hit a number, they'll do it by any means necessary.  But.

75
00:07:17,920 --> 00:07:21,280
But if you want honest behavior, you need to reward honest performance.

76
00:07:22,080 --> 00:07:28,160
Traditional budget based incentives may appear to drive performance, but in reality they often encourage manipulation.

77
00:07:28,560 --> 00:07:31,360
Rewarding timing tactics over true results.

78
00:07:32,080 --> 00:07:38,480
These behaviors distort internal data leading to unreliable forecasts and misaligned decisions about across departments.

79
00:07:38,720 --> 00:07:46,240
Over time, the cost of manipulation compounds plans build on biased inputs falter and and trust between teams erode.

80
00:07:46,560 --> 00:07:55,680
In contrast, linear incentive systems reward every increment of value created, promoting honest forecasting, consistent effort and strategic alignment.

81
00:07:55,840 --> 00:08:02,960
When performance is rewarded continuously, behavior naturally shifts toward long term thinking and company wide success.

82
00:08:04,480 --> 00:08:06,240
And that's it.  Hope you've enjoyed it.